    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>The Texas homestead exemption removes part of your home's value from property taxation, and as of 2026 it is bigger than it has ever been: $140,000 off your school district taxable value, or $200,000 if you are 65 or older or disabled. It costs nothing to file, you usually only file once, and in a high-tax area like Houston it can save a typical homeowner well over a thousand dollars a year.</p>
<p>Below is what changed in 2025, who qualifies, how to apply with the Harris Central Appraisal District (HCAD) or your county's district, and a worked example of the savings.</p>

<h2>Texas homestead exemption amounts for 2026</h2>
<p>Texas voters approved two constitutional amendments on November 4, 2025, that raised the school district homestead exemptions. Proposition 13 raised the general exemption from $100,000 to $140,000. Proposition 11 raised the extra exemption for owners who are 65 or older or disabled from $10,000 to $60,000. Both took effect for the 2025 tax year, so they carry through to 2026 bills.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Exemption</th><th>Applies to</th><th>Amount (2026)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>General residence homestead</td><td>School district taxes</td><td>$140,000 of appraised value (required by state law)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Age 65+ or disabled (additional)</td><td>School district taxes</td><td>$60,000 more, for $200,000 total</td></tr>
<tr><td>Local option percentage</td><td>County, city, special districts that adopt it</td><td>Up to 20% of appraised value; minimum $5,000 when adopted</td></tr>
<tr><td>Local option 65+ / disabled</td><td>Any taxing unit that adopts it</td><td>At least $3,000; amount set locally</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The state amounts are confirmed on the <a href="https://comptroller.texas.gov/taxes/property-tax/exemptions/">Texas Comptroller's property tax exemptions page</a>. Local amounts vary widely. Harris County and the City of Houston, for example, both offer the 20% optional homestead exemption, while other cities and utility districts may offer less or nothing. Your appraisal district's account page lists every exemption applied to your home and each taxing unit's share.</p>
<p>One note on disability: you can claim the over-65 exemption or the disability exemption for school taxes, but not both at once. If you qualify for both, you pick one.</p>

<h2>Who qualifies for a homestead exemption in Texas</h2>
<p>The rules are simpler than most people expect. To qualify for the general residence homestead exemption you must:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Own the home.</strong> You can own it directly or through a qualifying trust. A home held in an LLC or corporation does not qualify.</li>
<li><strong>Live in it as your principal residence.</strong> You can only claim one homestead, in Texas or anywhere else.</li>
<li><strong>Show a Texas ID with the same address.</strong> The appraisal district generally requires a Texas driver's license or state ID card whose address matches the property.</li>
</ul>
<p>The homestead can include up to 20 acres of land used for residential purposes, and it covers condos, townhomes and manufactured homes. A rental property or a second home does not qualify. Investors buying a duplex or fourplex can usually claim the exemption only on the unit they live in.</p>
<h3>The year-you-buy rule</h3>
<p>Exemptions are normally based on your status on January 1. Homebuyers get an exception: if you buy after January 1, you can receive the general homestead exemption for the part of the year you own and live in the home, as long as the seller did not already receive the same exemption for that year. You must apply before the first anniversary of your purchase.</p>
<p>In practice, many sellers already had a homestead, so the exemption stays on the account for the year of sale and your own exemption starts on January 1 of the next year. Either way, file as soon as you move in so nothing falls through the cracks.</p>

<h2>The 10% homestead appraisal cap</h2>
<p>A homestead exemption also unlocks the 10% appraisal cap. Once your home qualifies, the appraised value used for taxes cannot rise more than 10% per year, plus the value of any new improvements such as an addition. Routine repairs and maintenance do not count as new improvements.</p>
<p>The cap starts on January 1 of the tax year after you first qualify, so it will not help in your first year of ownership. It matters a lot in a rising market. If HCAD's market value for your home jumps 18% in one year, your taxable appraised value can still only climb 10%. The cap stays as long as you or your spouse keep the homestead, and it ends when the home sells.</p>
<p>The cap limits your appraised value, not your tax bill. If tax rates rise or an exemption is removed, your bill can still go up.</p>

<h2>Over-65 and disabled homeowners: extra exemptions and the tax ceiling</h2>
<p>Owners who are 65 or older, or who meet the Social Security definition of disabled, get three layers of help:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>The extra $60,000 school exemption</strong>, for $200,000 in total.</li>
<li><strong>Any local over-65 or disability exemption</strong> your county, city or other taxing unit has adopted.</li>
<li><strong>A school tax ceiling.</strong> Your school district taxes cannot go above the amount imposed in the first year you qualified, except for new improvements you add.</li>
</ol>
<p>The ceiling is not frozen forever at the original number. Texas law lowers it when the state compresses school tax rates or raises homestead exemptions, so the 2025 increases reduced the ceilings of existing senior and disabled homeowners too. Counties, cities and junior college districts may adopt their own ceilings as well.</p>
<p>The over-65 exemption is not automatic. You must apply, but you can do so in the year you turn 65, and it applies to that entire tax year. A surviving spouse who is at least 55 when the qualifying spouse dies can generally keep the over-65 benefits by applying.</p>

<h2>How to file: Form 50-114 and your appraisal district</h2>
<p>You file with your county appraisal district, not the state and not the tax office. The application is Comptroller Form 50-114, Application for Residence Homestead Exemption. Many districts, including HCAD, offer their own version and an online option.</p>
<h3>Filing in Harris County</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mobile app:</strong> HCAD lets you file through its HCAD Info and Exemptions app. You need the date you moved in and a photo of the front and back of your Texas driver's license. See the <a href="https://hcad.org/hcad-online-services/homestead/">HCAD homestead page</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Online, mail or in person:</strong> HCAD also accepts the paper form by mail or at its office. There is no fee.</li>
<li><strong>Recently purchased homes:</strong> If HCAD still shows the seller as owner, include a copy of your recorded deed.</li>
</ul>
<p>Fort Bend, Montgomery, Brazoria, Galveston and other surrounding counties have their own appraisal districts and online portals, but the form and rules are the same statewide.</p>
<h3>Deadlines and late filing</h3>
<p>The standard deadline is before May 1 of the tax year. If you miss it, the chief appraiser must still accept a late application filed up to two years after the delinquency date for that year's taxes. In Texas, taxes generally become delinquent on February 1 of the following year. If your late application is approved after you paid, the tax collector refunds the tax on the exempted amount without a separate refund request.</p>
<p>Once granted, the exemption generally stays in place and you do not reapply each year, unless the district asks you to. Check your account every spring when the notice of appraised value arrives, because exemptions occasionally drop off after a refinance or a title change.</p>

<h2>Worked example: what the exemption saves</h2>
<p>Here is a simple illustration for a $400,000 Houston-area home. The tax rates are round numbers for the math, not your actual rates.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Taxing unit</th><th>Exemption</th><th>Value removed</th><th>Illustrative rate</th><th>Annual savings</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>School district</td><td>$140,000 general</td><td>$140,000</td><td>1.00%</td><td>$1,400</td></tr>
<tr><td>County</td><td>20% optional</td><td>$80,000</td><td>0.40%</td><td>$320</td></tr>
<tr><td>City</td><td>20% optional</td><td>$80,000</td><td>0.50%</td><td>$400</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td><strong>$2,120</strong></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>If the same owner is 65 or older, the school exemption grows to $200,000, adding another $600 a year at a 1.00% rate before any local senior exemption or the school tax ceiling. Over a decade of ownership, plus the 10% cap in rising years, the savings add up to real money.</p>
<p>Plug in your own value and your actual tax rates with our <a href="/tools/texas-property-tax-calculator/">Texas property tax calculator</a> to see your numbers.</p>
