<h2>Common homestead exemption mistakes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Assuming the title company filed it.</strong> It usually does not. Filing is your job.</li>
<li><strong>Forgetting to update your driver's license.</strong> A mismatched address is a common reason for delay or denial.</li>
<li><strong>Keeping an old homestead.</strong> When you move, the exemption on your previous home should end. Claiming two homesteads can lead to back taxes, penalties and interest.</li>
<li><strong>Not applying at 65.</strong> The senior exemption and ceiling are separate from the general exemption and must be requested.</li>
<li><strong>Skipping the value protest.</strong> The exemption reduces taxable value, but a high appraisal still costs you. Pair it with a protest each year if the value looks wrong. Our guide to <a href="/blog/protest-property-taxes-harris-county/">protesting property taxes in Harris County</a> walks through the process.</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep your approval letter, deed and closing disclosure together in one place. You will want them if the exemption is ever questioned or when you sell.</p>
[[product:accordion-file-organizer]]
<p>Exemption rules have special cases for disabled veterans, surviving spouses, trusts and inherited property, so confirm your situation with your appraisal district or a CPA or property tax attorney.</p>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>If you own and live in your Texas home, file for the homestead exemption. In 2026 it removes $140,000 from your school taxable value, $200,000 if you are 65 or older or disabled, and it starts the 10% appraisal cap the following year. Filing with HCAD takes a few minutes on your phone, and if you missed past years you may still be able to claim refunds. Buying soon? Start with our <a href="/blog/first-time-home-buyer-texas/">first-time home buyer guide for Texas</a>, and browse more <a href="/category/homeownership/">homeownership guides</a>.</p>
